            as a countably affine sum of elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i\in \mathbb{N}} p_i a_i
            to refer to a countably affine sum dropping the explicit reference to the condition that the limit of partial sums
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mi N
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i=0}^N p_i
            converges to one. An alternative notation to the countable affine sum notation is to use the integral notation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
