                        %mi K
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi 𝒫
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta_K \circ \mu_K = \beta_K \circ \mathcal{P}\beta_K
            %p The method employed in this example is not restricted to locally convex compact Hausdorff spaces. It shows that the algebras of a probability monad are a superconvex space. That implication is the motivation for the next example.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+convex+space"} totally convex space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+space"} convex space
          %h2#references References
          %p The notion of superconvex spaces originates with:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dieter+Pumpl%C3%BCn"}> Dieter Pumplün
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Banach spaces and superconvex modules
              = succeed "," do
                %em Symposia Gaussiana – Conference A: Mathematics and Theoretical Physics
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9783110886726.323"} doi:10.1515/9783110886726.323
          %p
            The proof that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SCvx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{SCvx}
            has no cogenerator is due to:
          %ul
            %li{:id => "BörgerKemper94a"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhard+B%C3%B6rger"}> Reinhard Börger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Kemper"} Ralf Kemper
              = succeed "," do
                %em There is no cogenerator for total convex spaces
              %strong 35
              4 (1994) 335-338 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1994__35_4_335_0"}> numdam:CTGDC_1994__35_4_335_0
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi SCvx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{SCvx}
            is a symmetric monoidal argument can be proven the same way it is for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convex+spaces"} convex spaces
            simply by replacing the finite affine sums with countable affine sums. That proof was given by
          %ul
            %li#Meng
              Xiao-qing Meng,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories of convex sets and of metric spaces with applications to stochastic programming and related areas
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Meng.djvu", :title => "Ph.D. thesis of Meng"} djvu
          %p Proposition 1.2 in
          %ul
            %li{:id => "BörgerKemper94b"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Reinhard+B%C3%B6rger"}> Reinhard Börger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ralf+Kemper"} Ralf Kemper
              = succeed "," do
                %em Cogenerators for convex spaces
              %strong 2
              (1994) 1-11 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00878499"}> doi:10.1007/BF00878499
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            is particularly useful for viewing superconvex spaces as positively convex spaces which are somewhat easier to work with because the condition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{i \in \mathbb{N}}p_i=1
            is replaced by the inequality
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le 1
          %p
            Superconvex spaces play a role in obtaining a factorization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giry+monad"} Giry monad
            yielding
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirk+Sturtz"}> Kirk Sturtz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Deriving the Giry algebras on standard Borel spaces using
                  = succeed "-generalized" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi ℝ
                            %mn ∞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}_{\infty}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14861"} arXiv:2409.14861
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p For purposes of constructing models of complex systems using superconvex spaces the construction given in Example 6.1 of the following article applies equally well to superconvex spaces.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tobias+Fritz"}> Tobias Fritz
              ,
              %em Convex spaces I: definition and examples
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0903.5522"}> arXiv/0903.5522
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            The term
            %em strong convex space
            was employed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/George+Mackey"}> George Mackey
              , p. 68 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em The Mathematical Foundations of Quantum Mechanics: a Lecture-note Volume
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
